 "operation": [
  {
   "topic": "Light sensing and ADC",
   "page": 8,
   "text": "Two photodiode arrays turn light into current, which a charge-balancing integrating ADC converts to an unsigned count of up to 16 bits. The integration time comes from the internal oscillator and the selected 4, 8, 12 or 16-bit counter, so resolution trades against speed. Range 0 is the setting for very dim light. The part can be set to read either visible light (eye-like response) or infrared."
  },
  {
   "topic": "Operating modes and power-down",
   "page": 8,
   "text": "After power is applied the part sits in power-down with its data registers at 0. A one-shot command runs one conversion (ALS or IR), then the part powers itself down and holds the result for the host to read whenever it likes. A continuous command keeps converting and refreshing the data registers until a power-down command arrives. Powered down, supply current is 0.24 µA typical, 0.51 µA maximum."
  },
  {
   "topic": "Interrupt",
   "page": 8,
   "text": "INT is an open-drain active-low output that flags when the light reading goes above the upper threshold or below the lower one (at or beyond either threshold counts). Conversions carry on after INT asserts, so a host wanting the count that triggered it must read before the next conversion overwrites the data registers. The persistence setting requires 1, 4, 8 or 16 consecutive out-of-window conversions before an interrupt, which rejects short events such as a camera flash (8 cycles is suggested for that). The INT pin and the status bit B2 of COMMAND-I stay set until COMMAND-I (0x00) is read over I2C; that read clears both."
  },
  {
   "topic": "Thresholds",
   "page": 13,
   "text": "The lower threshold is the 16-bit value in 0x05 (high byte) and 0x04 (low byte), default 0x0000; the upper threshold is 0x07 (high byte) and 0x06 (low byte), default 0xFFFF."
  },
  {
   "topic": "Range and resolution",
   "page": 12,
   "text": "COMMAND-II bits B1:B0 pick full scale of 1,000, 4,000, 16,000 or 64,000 lux; a lower range gives finer lux per count. Bits B3:B2 pick the ADC width, which sets both resolution and integration time (105 ms at 16 bits, 6.5 ms at 12, 0.41 ms at 8, 0.0256 ms at 4). Only the 16-bit setting rejects 50/60 Hz flicker well. Data are in 0x02 (low byte) and 0x03 (high byte), refreshed after every conversion, using D0 up to D3, D7, D11 or D15 as the width dictates."
  },
  {
   "topic": "Flicker rejection",
   "page": 14,
   "text": "Lamps on 50 Hz or 60 Hz mains flicker at the line frequency. An integrating ADC averages out periodic noise whose period divides its integration time evenly, so an integration time that is a whole number of mains cycles (k × 16.66 ms for 60 Hz) removes it. The 105 ms 16-bit conversion rejects both 50 Hz and 60 Hz at once."
  },
  {
   "topic": "Device ID and brownout",
   "page": 13,
   "text": "Register 0x0F holds the device ID in bits B5:B3, which read 101 (pattern xx101xxx), and the brownout flag BOUT in B7. Bits B0 to B2 and B6 are reserved and may change, so mask them when checking the ID. BOUT reads 1 after power-up, meaning the part may have seen a brownout; the host writes it to 0 while setting the part up. The register reads 0xA8 at power-on."
  },
  {
   "topic": "Power-on reset",
   "page": 10,
   "text": "Above the POR trip level the part comes up in standby; when VDD falls below it the part goes to reset. This two-way POR keeps the logic from starting in a wrong state before the internal bandgap settles and blocks register access during power-up, guarding against brownout corruption. A START condition sent during power-up is ignored."
  },
  {
   "topic": "I2C interface",
   "page": 9,
   "text": "The part is an I2C slave. Its 7-bit device address is fixed at 1000100, followed by the R/W bit (1 for read, 0 for write), so the address byte is 0x88 to write and 0x89 to read. Bytes go MSB first. SDA must be stable while SCL is high; START is SDA falling with SCL high and STOP is SDA rising with SCL high. The part ACKs a valid address byte, the register address byte and each written data byte; the master ACKs each byte it reads except the last. A STOP mid-byte, or before a whole data byte and its ACK, resets the interface without doing the read or write and leaves the registers as they were. Both bus lines need pull-ups to the bus supply."
  },
  {
   "topic": "Writes and reads",
   "page": 9,
   "text": "Byte write: START, device address (write), register address, data, STOP. The part then performs an internal write; during it the inputs are ignored and SDA is high impedance. Burst write carries on with more data bytes, the address stepping up by one after each and wrapping from the last register back to the first; the pointer stays at the last address written. Byte read: START, device address (write), register address, repeated START, device address (read), then one data byte that the master leaves unacknowledged before STOP. Burst read is the same, but the master ACKs each byte to get the next."
  },
  {
   "topic": "Board mounting",
   "page": 14,
   "text": "Heating and cooling of the board bends it slightly and stresses the die in the ODFN package; where the board twists, accuracy can suffer. A spot near a board edge, or on the short side, sees the least bending."
  },
  {
   "topic": "Layout",
   "page": 14,
   "text": "The part is relatively insensitive to layout. Keep the supply and I2C traces well clear of noise sources, and fit two decoupling capacitors, 1 µF and 0.1 µF, close to the part."
  },
  {
   "topic": "Soldering",
   "page": 14,
   "text": "Reflow by convection, not direct infrared heating. A standard lead-free profile peaking at +260°C suits it; no custom profile is needed. Before designing the footprint, consult TB477 on ODFN assembly."
  },
  {
   "topic": "Sensor location",
   "page": 15,
   "text": "The light-sensitive area is 0.38 mm wide and 0.28 mm tall, centred between the two rows of terminals and offset 0.30 mm from the package centre toward pins 3 and 4."
  }
 ],
